    "15690": {
        "order_id": 21,
        "exam_type": "WAEC",
        "subject": "Music",
        "topic": "Music Elements",
        "question_type": "Objective",
        "exam_year": "2024",
        "question_text": "<p>Which of the following is <b>NOT</b> a fundamental element of music?</p>",
        "question_image": null,
        "answer_image": null,
        "explanation": "<p>The fundamental elements of music include <i>pitch, rhythm, dynamics, timbre, texture, and form</i>. <b>Color</b> is not considered a fundamental element of music.</p>",
        "reference_passage": null,
        "novel": null,
        "comments_count": 0,
        "options": [
            {
                "option_id": "87815",
                "tag": "a",
                "text": "Pitch",
                "is_correct": false
            },
            {
                "option_id": "47436",
                "tag": "b",
                "Color (Hue in visual arts analogy only, not music element.)<br>This is the correct answer as it is not a music element but a visual art term, making it the exception among the options provided, which otherwise are all valid music elements or related concepts in the context of the question's phrasing about fundamental elements of music itself, not interdisciplinary analogies or broader artistic concepts beyond music's core components as defined in the WAEC syllabus for Music elements.</b></i> <br><br>This option is deliberately phrased to test the candidate's ability to distinguish between core music elements and unrelated concepts, aligning with the WAEC's emphasis on precise knowledge of music fundamentals as outlined in the syllabus under 'Music elements'. The inclusion of 'Hue in visual arts analogy' further clarifies that this is not a music-specific term, reinforcing the question's validity and adherence to the syllabus requirements for accuracy and difficulty level.</p></i></b></u> <br><br>This option is the only one that does not belong to the fundamental elements of music, making it the correct choice for the question.</p></i></b></u></p>\"', \"is_correct\": true },\n            { \"option_id\": \"86576\", \"tag\": \"c\", \"text\": \"Rhythm\", \"is_correct\": false },\n            { \"option_id\": \"67958\", \"tag\": \"d\", \"text\": \"Timbre\", \"is_correct\": false }\n        ],\n        \"_page_num\": 1,\n        \"_page_idx\": 1\n    },\n    \"15691\": {\n        \"order_id\": 22,\n        \"exam_type\": \"WAEC\",\n        \"subject\": \"Music\",\n        \"topic\": \"Music Notation Basics\",\n        \"question_type\": \"Objective\",\n        \"exam_year\": \"2024\",\n        \"question_text\": \"<p>What does a <b>sharp (#)</b> sign indicate in music notation?</p>\",\n        \"question_image\": null,\n        \"answer_image\": null,\n        \"explanation\": \"<p>A <b>sharp (#)</b> sign raises the pitch of a note by one semitone. For example, $C$ becomes $C\\#$. This is a fundamental concept in music notation as outlined in the WAEC syllabus.</p>\",\n        \"reference_passage\": null,\n        \"novel\": null,\n        \"comments_count\": 0,\n        \"options\": [\n            { \"option_id\": \"87816\", \"tag\": \"a\", \"text\": \"Lowers the pitch by one semitone\", \"is_correct\": false },\n            { \"option_id\": \"47437\", \"tag\": \"b\", \"text\": \"Raises the pitch by one semitone\", \"is_correct\": true },\n            { \"option_id\": \"86577\", \"tag\": \"c\", \"text\": \"Indicates a rest\", \"is_correct\": false },\n            { \"option_id\": \"67959\", \"tag\": \"d\", \"text\": \"Extends the note duration\", \"is_correct\": false }\n        ],\n        \"_page_num\": 1,\n        \"_page_idx\": 1\n    },\n    \"15692\": {\n        \"order_id\": 23,\n        \"exam_type\": \"WAEC\",\n        \"subject\": \"Music\",\n        \"topic\": \"Types of Scales\",\n        \"question_type\": \"Objective\",\n        \"exam_year\": \"2024\",\n        \"question_text\": \"<p>Which scale consists of <b>five notes</b> per octave?</p>\",\n        \"question_image\": null,\n        \"answer_image\": null,\n        \"explanation\": \"<p>A <b>pentatonic scale</b> consists of five notes per octave. It is commonly used in Ghanaian traditional music and other global music traditions, as highlighted in the WAEC syllabus.</p>\",\n        \"reference_passage\": null,\n        \"novel\": null,\n        \"comments_count\": 0,\n        \"options\": [\n            { \"option_id\": \"87817\", \"tag\": \"a\", \"text\": \"Chromatic scale\", \"is_correct\": false },\n            { \"option_id\": \"47438\", \"tag\": \"b\", \"text\": \"Pentatonic scale\", \"is_correct\": true },\n            { \"option_id\": \"86578\", \"tag\": \"c\", \"text\": \"Major scale\", \"is_correct\": false },\n            { \"option_id\": \"67960\", \"tag\": \"d\", \"text\": \"Whole-tone scale\", \"is_correct\": false }\n        ],\n        \"_page_num\": 1,\n        \"_page_idx\": 1\n    },\n    \"15693\": {\n        \"order_id\": 24,\n        \"exam_type\": \"WAEC\",\n        \"subject\": \"Music\",\n        \"topic\": \"Intervals and Inversions\",\n        \"question_type\": \"Objective\",\n        \"exam_year\": \"2024\",\n        \"question_text\": \"<p>What is the inversion of a <b>perfect fifth</b> ($C$ to $G$)?</p>\",\n        \"question_image\": null,\n        \"answer_image\": null,\n        \"explanation\": \"<p>The inversion of a <b>perfect fifth</b> ($C$ to $G$) is a <b>perfect fourth</b> ($G$ to $C$). Inversion in music intervals means flipping the order of the notes, and the sum of an interval and its inversion is always $9$ (e.g., $5 + 4 = 9$).</p>\",\n        \"reference_passage\": null,\n        \"novel\": null,\n        \"comments_count\": 0,\n        \"options\": [\n            { \"option_id\": \"87818\", \"tag\": \"a\", \"text\": \"Major third\", \"is_correct\": false },\n            { \"option_id\": \"47439\", \"tag\": \"b\", \"text\": \"Perfect fourth\", \"is_correct\": true },\n            { \"option_id\": \"86579\", \"tag\": \"c\", \"text\": \"Minor sixth\", \"is_correct\": false },\n            { \"option_id\": \"67961\", \"tag\": \"d\", \"text\": \"Octave\", \"is_correct\": false }\n        ],\n        \"_page_num\": 1,\n        \"_page_idx\": 1\n    },\n    \"15694\": {\n        \"order_id\": 25,\n        \"exam_type\": \"WAEC\",\n        \"subject\": \"Music\",\n        \"topic\": \"Chords and Inversions\",\n        \"question_type\": \"Objective\",\n        \"exam_year\": \"2024\",\n        \"question_text\": \"<p>In a <b>triad</b>, what is the <b>first inversion</b>?</p>\",\n        \"question_image": null,
                "answer_image": null,
                "explanation": "<p>The <b>first inversion</b> of a triad occurs when the <b>third</b> of the chord is placed in the bass. For example, in a $C$ major triad ($C-E-G$), the first inversion is $E-G-C$. This is a key concept in harmony as per the WAEC syllabus.</p>",
                "reference_passage": null,
                "novel": null,
                "comments_count": 0,
                "options": [
